WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.746
GUIPEUN KANG: So octopuses use its arm to explore.

00:00:02.746 --> 00:00:04.800
COREY ALLARD: And people have&nbsp;
wondered about this for a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.940
how they are using those arms to do this -&nbsp;
how they're finding things on the seafloor.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:12.960
VOICEOVER: Octopuses feel their&nbsp;
way to a tasty meal but for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:16.966
long time researchers have believed&nbsp;
there is more than touch going on.

00:00:16.966 --> 00:00:21.420
COREY ALLARD: Touch-taste senses is&nbsp;
the term that combines elements of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:24.420
mechanical sensitivity and&nbsp;
also chemical sensitivity.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:28.020
VOICEOVER: Now researchers&nbsp;
think they're uncovering the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:32.206
molecular mechanism behind this&nbsp;
proposed 'chemotactile' sense.

00:00:32.206 --> 00:00:36.840
COREY ALLARD: How does this apparent&nbsp;
chemotactile sense work? What are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:42.960
cells? What are the molecules? And how does&nbsp;
the animal use them to interact with its world?

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.300
VOICEOVER: And their work is opening a new window&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:50.266
into how sensory systems evolve and&nbsp;
how those systems impact behaviour.

00:00:50.266 --> 00:00:55.920
GUIPEUN KANG: Octopuses are excellent&nbsp;
model systems to understand how molecular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.820
evolution affects the sensory systems of animals.

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:08.026
VOICEOVER: Octopuses experience the&nbsp;
world very differently from humans.

00:01:08.026 --> 00:01:09.720
COREY ALLARD: Certainly there are&nbsp;
some things that are familiar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:14.580
we know that octopus are visual&nbsp;
animals and they'll use their eyes, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:20.820
what's really striking about them is the way they&nbsp;
use those sort of enigmatic eight arms that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:26.220
have - and they're using those to really reach&nbsp;
into cracks in the sea floor to probe around to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:32.100
surfaces or areas that aren't accessible to things&nbsp;
like their eyes that maybe they can't see into.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:38.700
VOICEOVER: Octopuses appear to be able to accept&nbsp;
or reject potential prey using only their arms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:43.966
and it was this behaviour that led researchers&nbsp;
to investigate their sensory capabilities.

00:01:43.966 --> 00:01:49.320
COREY ALLARD: There were some very early studies&nbsp;
from the 1960s that showed what looked like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:54.720
sensory cells in the epithelium of the sucker&nbsp;
cups and that was one of the things that got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:58.620
us curious in the first place that - yeah it&nbsp;
seems like maybe there's a bona fide sensory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:04.200
system housed within the arms and the sucker cups,&nbsp;
but we don't know anything about how it works.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:09.420
VOICEOVER: But the publication of the first&nbsp;
octopus genome in 2015 has led to new studies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:14.266
including research into gene expression&nbsp;
profiles in different parts of the octopus.

00:02:14.266 --> 00:02:18.240
COREY ALLARD: The rationale here is that the&nbsp;
sensory receptors we're looking for should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:23.940
more abundant in the rims of the sucker&nbsp;
cups versus other places in the octopus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:30.240
And what was really striking to us was&nbsp;
that instead of a single receptor or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:34.500
couple receptors we identified an entire&nbsp;
family of these chemotactile receptors.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:40.080
VOICEOVER: But the receptors they identified&nbsp;
which they call chemotactile reception or CRs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:44.866
didn't look like the kind of receptors&nbsp;
usually seen in sensory systems.

00:02:44.866 --> 00:02:50.400
COREY ALLARD: They didn't resemble known sensory&nbsp;
receptors instead they're most closely related to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:55.320
a family of receptors called&nbsp;
nicotinic acetylcholine receptors.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:59.460
VOICEOVER: Nicotinic acetylcholine&nbsp;
receptors are found throughout the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:04.980
nervous system of many different animals but&nbsp;
usually they detect neurotransmitters within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:09.180
the body and are involved with things&nbsp;
like controlling muscle contractions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:14.880
In humans that a focus of research into&nbsp;
treatments for everything from Parkinson's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:21.526
disease to nicotine addiction but they are not&nbsp;
sensory receptors or so researchers thought.

00:03:21.526 --> 00:03:24.660
COREY ALLARD: In the course of their&nbsp;
evolution octopus have converted these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:29.820
ancestral nicotinic acetylcholine receptors&nbsp;
into sensory receptors they look almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:34.740
identical at first glance and it wasn't until&nbsp;
we looked closer that we started to appreciate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:38.880
some of the structural regions that are&nbsp;
mediating these functional differences

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:44.700
VOICEOVER: The team imaged the octopus receptors&nbsp;
using cryo-electron microscopy and compared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:49.740
structure they found with the nicotinic receptors&nbsp;
found commonly in other animals and they found a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:55.740
subtle but very important difference in an area&nbsp;
called the ligand binding site. This is where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.740 --> 00:04:01.200
the receptor binds with its chemical partner.&nbsp;
In nicotinic receptors this is a cage-like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:06.300
structure that binds with neurotransmitters&nbsp;
like acetylcholine causing its ion channel to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:12.780
open and transmit signals, but the octopus&nbsp;
receptors are different. Instead of a cage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:17.820
they've evolved to have a shallow groove and&nbsp;
that allows for a wider range of materials to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:22.980
bind in particular materials that don't&nbsp;
mix well with water like the kinds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:28.726
greasy residues left on the sea floor by&nbsp;
potential prey, a useful tool in a hunt.

00:04:28.726 --> 00:04:35.700
COREY ALLARD: We describe this as being a&nbsp;
chemo tactile sense which basically combines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:40.260
chemo sensation sensation of chemicals&nbsp;
who have tactile sensation or touch.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:44.100
VOICEOVER: This clarified picture of&nbsp;
the molecular mechanism behind taste&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:49.920
by touch helps explain some of the octopus's&nbsp;
unique behaviours - especially when compared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:53.926
to the structures of the same types of&nbsp;
receptor in related animals like squid.

00:04:53.926 --> 00:04:59.400
GUIPEUN KANG: Octopuses use their chemotactile&nbsp;
receptors for their exploratory predation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:07.200
behaviour. Interestingly octopus and&nbsp;
squid have a different predatory strategy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:14.220
instead of using its arms for exploration,&nbsp;
rather squid - ambush predator - hides,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:20.460
strike and pull their potential prey&nbsp;
and then decide to eat or reject it.

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:25.380
VOICEOVER: This behavioural difference, the&nbsp;
researchers say, can be explained by their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:31.066
different receptors. For example the octopus&nbsp;
receptors wouldn't work in the open water.

00:05:31.066 --> 00:05:36.660
COREY ALLARD: The types of chemicals that&nbsp;
they're actually able to sense are very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:42.600
hydrophobic molecules things that you would&nbsp;
not expect to find in the water column in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:47.280
marine environment and things that instead&nbsp;
you would expect to find on the surfaces in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:51.120
a marine environment, so it makes sense that&nbsp;
they would have to physically touch them.

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:52.800
GUIPEUN KANG: On the contrary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:57.180
the squid chemotactile receptor&nbsp;
binding site, is not responding to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:05.220
greasy molecules. Instead it responds to a&nbsp;
bitter tastents, that are soluble and easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:12.960
diffusive in water. It explains squid's&nbsp;
strikingly different predatory behaviour.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:17.520
VOICEOVER: Evolutionary differences at&nbsp;
a molecular level align with strikingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:22.305
different abilities and behaviours. And&nbsp;
this is far from the end of the story

00:06:22.305 --> 00:06:26.940
GUIPEUN KANGE: Oh yeah, there are there&nbsp;
are a lot of things we don't understand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:32.520
here we just started look at one&nbsp;
of each receptor from octopus and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:37.920
squid there are more octopus and&nbsp;
squid chemotactile receptors with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:43.740
different properties [they] may pursue&nbsp;
different functions in sensory system.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:48.900
VOICEOVER: For example, there is evidence&nbsp;
that some octopus receptors can also detect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:55.500
voltage but how this ability fits into their&nbsp;
broader sensory system is still unknown even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:59.806
within the world of the chemotactile receptors&nbsp;
already studied there's much more to learn.

00:06:59.806 --> 00:07:04.020
COREY ALLARD: You know we initially got&nbsp;
into this study thinking that we would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:09.060
be studying a sensory system that's&nbsp;
used for predation for finding prey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:14.100
and while we still think that's the case so&nbsp;
far most of what we've discovered relates to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:19.860
perversive stimuli or things that the octopus&nbsp;
doesn't like and so we're really interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:26.460
to find if somewhere in this diversity of CRs&nbsp;
there are receptors that mediate what we would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:30.600
call appetitive sensation or, you know, food&nbsp;
- things that the octopus maybe wants to eat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:37.740
Or if the octopus maybe just eats anything unless&nbsp;
it has a signal that says no don't eat this.

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:41.520
VOICEOVER: Researchers are gradually&nbsp;
building a better comprehension of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:47.400
how sensory systems evolve and being able to&nbsp;
combine behavioural observation with complex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:52.740
structural understanding give scientists yet&nbsp;
another piece in the evolutionary puzzle.

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:57.900
GUIPEUN KANG: Cephalopods, including nautilus&nbsp;
and cuttlefish - they're evolving differently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:04.320
from octopus and squids, so that would&nbsp;
be very interesting to see how and why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:10.740
they're different from a molecular,&nbsp;
atomic level to animal behaviour level.

